{
  "id": "05e56670-48e2-480e-85f3-7148e2bde57e",
  "state": "COMPLETED",
  "response": "# Independent referee report: Erdős Problem 99 and the n=9n=9n=9 certificate

Verdict: The diametral-anchor method and the proposed clique obstruction are mathematically sound in principle, and they can certify an unrestricted global lower bound. However, the reported counts of 1,348 surviving parents, 434,876 refined edges, and zero K7K_7K7​'s do not, by themselves, establish that the implementation satisfies the necessary covering and graph-construction conditions.

I identify below the exact conditions under which the certificate is valid, the principal ways it could fail, and the remaining verification gap. I do not treat the reported graph computation as independently reproduced.

The proposed conclusion is

D9>243100=2.43,\boxed{D_9>\frac{243}{100}=2.43,}D9​>100243​=2.43,​

where

Dn=min⁡P⊂R2∣P∣=nmin⁡p≠q∣p−q∣≥1diam⁡(P).D_n=\min_{\substack{P\subset\mathbb R^2\\|P|=n\\ \min_{p\ne q}|p-q|\ge1}}\operatorname{diam}(P).Dn​=P⊂R2∣P∣=nminp=q​∣p−q∣≥1​min​diam(P).

This concerns every unrestricted nine-point configuration, not just configurations with a prescribed convex hull, symmetry, contact graph, or local optimality property.

The distinction from the original problem is essential: [Erdős Problem 99](https://www.erdosproblems.com/99?utm_source=chatgpt.com) asks whether every global optimizer has an exact unit equilateral triangle for all sufficiently large nnn. It remains listed as open. A rigorous lower bound on D9D_9D9​, however strong, cannot settle that eventual statement. Erdős Problems+1

## 1. Published mathematical baseline

The directly relevant published results are:

- Erdős (1994): [Some Problems in Number Theory, Combinatorics and Combinatorial Geometry](https://mathematica-pannonica.ttk.pte.hu/articles/mp05-2/mp05-2-261-269.pdf?utm_source=chatgpt.com). Original source of the conjectural asymptotic picture.
- Bezdek–Fodor (1999): [Minimal Diameter of Certain Sets in the Plane](https://doi.org/10.1006/jcta.1998.2889?utm_source=chatgpt.com). Establishes D8=1/(2sin⁡(π/14))D_8=1/(2\sin(\pi/14))D8​=1/(2sin(π/14)), with nonunique optimal configurations. ScienceDirect+1
- Daróczy-Kiss (2005): [A New Bound for the Minimal Diameter of Nine Coplanar Congruent Disks](https://ac.inf.elte.hu/Vol_025_2005/doi/025_25.pdf?utm_source=chatgpt.com). Gives D9<2.5693D_9<2.5693D9​<2.5693 using an explicit nine-point construction, not a global characterization. ac.inf.elte.hu

Thus the reported project bound, if fully certified, would combine with the published construction to give

2.43<D9<2.5693.\boxed{2.43<D_9<2.5693.}2.43<D9​<2.5693.​

This would be a genuine global lower-bound advance, but it would not identify the exact value or any exact nine-point optimizer.

## 2. Diametral anchoring is valid without a symmetry assumption

Suppose, for contradiction, that nine distinct points have mutual distances at least 111 and diameter d≤243/100d\le 243/100d≤243/100.

Choose two original points attaining the diameter ddd. Translate, rotate and uniformly scale the configuration so those points become

A=(0,0),B=(1215,0).A=(0,0),\qquad B=(1215,0).A=(0,0),B=(1215,0).

The scaling factor is 1215/d1215/d1215/d. Every original separation consequently becomes at least

1215d≥1215243/100=500.\frac{1215}{d}\ge \frac{1215}{243/100}=500.d1215​≥243/1001215​=500.

Thus all nine scaled points must satisfy

500≤∣p−q∣≤1215(p≠q).(1)500\le |p-q|\le1215\qquad(p\ne q). \tag{1}500≤∣p−q∣≤1215(p=q).(1)

In particular, the seven non-anchor points are constrained by both anchors, but are otherwise unrestricted.

For p=(x,y)p=(x,y)p=(x,y), the two diameter conditions are

x2+y2≤12152,x^2+y^2\le1215^2,x2+y2≤12152,

(x−1215)2+y2≤12152.(x-1215)^2+y^2\le1215^2.(x−1215)2+y2≤12152.

These imply

0≤x≤1215,∣y∣≤321215<1053.0\le x\le1215,\qquad |y|\le\frac{\sqrt3}{2}\,1215<1053.0≤x≤1215,∣y∣≤23​​1215<1053.

Consequently every possible non-anchor point lies inside

R=[0,1230]×[−1080,1080].(2)R=[0,1230]\times[-1080,1080]. \tag{2}R=[0,1230]×[−1080,1080].(2)

No restriction to one side of the diametral line is made. This matters because the seven other points may occur on both sides.

Partition RRR into closed 30×3030\times3030×30 squares

Pij=[30i,30(i+1)]×[30j,30(j+1)],P_{ij}=[30i,30(i+1)]\times[30j,30(j+1)],Pij​=[30i,30(i+1)]×[30j,30(j+1)],

with

0≤i≤40,−36≤j≤35.(3)0\le i\le40,\qquad -36\le j\le35. \tag{3}0≤i≤40,−36≤j≤35.(3)

Their interiors are disjoint, and their union covers RRR, including all boundaries.

Each square has diameter 302<50030\sqrt2<500302​<500, so no two non-anchor sites can belong to the same selected parent square.

Boundary overlaps cause no problem: each site can be assigned to any one square containing it. No measure-theoretic disjointness lemma is needed.

## 3. Exact box-distance bounds

For intervals I=[a,b]I=[a,b]I=[a,b] and J=[c,d]J=[c,d]J=[c,d], define

δ(I,J)=max⁡(0,c−b,a−d)\delta(I,J)=\max(0,c-b,a-d)δ(I,J)=max(0,c−b,a−d)

and

Δ(I,J)=max⁡(∣a−d∣,∣b−c∣).\Delta(I,J)=\max(|a-d|,|b-c|).Δ(I,J)=max(∣a−d∣,∣b−c∣).

These are respectively the minimum and maximum possible absolute differences between elements of III and JJJ.

For axis-aligned rectangles Q=Ix×IyQ=I_x\times I_yQ=Ix​×Iy​ and Q′=Jx×JyQ'=J_x\times J_yQ′=Jx​×Jy​, the exact extreme squared distances are

m(Q,Q′)=δ(Ix,Jx)2+δ(Iy,Jy)2,m(Q,Q')= \delta(I_x,J_x)^2+\delta(I_y,J_y)^2,m(Q,Q′)=δ(Ix​,Jx​)2+δ(Iy​,Jy​)2,

M(Q,Q′)=Δ(Ix,Jx)2+Δ(Iy,Jy)2.M(Q,Q')= \Delta(I_x,J_x)^2+\Delta(I_y,J_y)^2.M(Q,Q′)=Δ(Ix​,Jx​)2+Δ(Iy​,Jy​)2.

The two coordinate extrema can be realized simultaneously because the rectangles are Cartesian products.

If QQQ and Q′Q'Q′ contain two actual sites satisfying (1), it is necessary that

M(Q,Q′)≥5002,m(Q,Q′)≤12152.(4)\boxed{M(Q,Q')\ge500^2,\qquad m(Q,Q')\le1215^2.} \tag{4}M(Q,Q′)≥5002,m(Q,Q′)≤12152.​(4)

Notice the direction of these inequalities.

The upper bound on possible distances must reach at least 500; the lower bound on possible distances must not exceed 1215. Reversing either condition can wrongly eliminate a genuine configuration.

Likewise, a square QQQ containing a non-anchor site must satisfy, for each anchor C∈{A,B}C\in\{A,B\}C∈{A,B},

M(Q,{C})≥5002,m(Q,{C})≤12152.(5)M(Q,\{C\})\ge500^2,\qquad m(Q,\{C\})\le1215^2. \tag{5}M(Q,{C})≥5002,m(Q,{C})≤12152.(5)

These are necessary tests, not assertions that every point in a surviving square is feasible.

Applying (5) to the specified parent grid gives exactly 1,348 surviving squares in my independent integer computation.

