### Why the clique pruning is exact

The coloring routine repeatedly constructs independent sets. Each such set receives one color, yielding a valid proper coloring of the candidate graph.

If ccc colors suffice for a candidate subgraph, that subgraph cannot contain a clique of size greater than ccc.

The recursion also removes each processed vertex from its current candidate set. Hence every possible clique is considered in some branch with its remaining vertices drawn from a strictly smaller candidate set.

Because vertices are processed in reverse, nondecreasing color order, the condition

depth+color[i]<7\text{depth}+\text{color}[i]<7depth+color[i]<7

correctly excludes the entire remaining prefix: it contains too few color classes to extend the current partial clique to size seven.

The other pruning condition,

depth+∣P∣<7,\text{depth}+|P|<7,depth+∣P∣<7,

is immediate.

Neither pruning condition is heuristic.

All geometric comparisons use squared integers. The integer magnitudes are safely within JavaScript's exact-integer range, and the graph operations use 32-bit bitsets.

