## 6. What the argument does not prove

The certificate proves that all nine-point unit-separated configurations have diameter strictly greater than 2.431. It therefore applies to unrestricted global minimizers, but it does not identify those minimizers or determine whether they contain unit triangles.

It also gives no implication for an unbounded sequence of nnn.

This distinction matters because Erdős's 1994 problem explicitly concerns every exact minimizer for all sufficiently large nnn, and an infinite sequence of counterexamples would be required to disprove it. Erdős states this in §3.2, page 268 of his original paper. Mathematica Pannonica

There is already a finite obstruction to carelessly generalizing the desired conclusion. Bezdek and Fodor proved the exact eight-point optimum

D8=12sin⁡(π/14)≈2.2469796.D_8=\frac1{2\sin(\pi/14)}\approx2.2469796.D8​=2sin(π/14)1​≈2.2469796.

A unit-side regular heptagon together with its center realizes that diameter. Its center-to-vertex distance is 1/(2sin⁡(π/7))>11/(2\sin(\pi/7))>11/(2sin(π/7))>1, so this particular exact global minimizer contains no unit equilateral triangle. The paper determines D8D_8D8​, not an eventual theorem for arbitrary nnn. ScienceDirect

The other previously identified approaches do not close that gap. Nonnegative KKT equilibrium stresses express stationarity, not necessarily unit triangles. Asymptotically circular or triangular-lattice-like configurations need not contain exact unit contacts. Eppstein's triangle-free penny-graph edge upper bound constrains contact graphs but does not supply the necessary complementary lower bound on the contact count of every unrestricted global minimizer. Journal of Graph Algorithms and Applications

The present finite certificate avoids these particular logical failures altogether.

